    "q-337307": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337307",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$30t+20$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">.</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
